ckages/lean"}, {"title": "figshare.com", "url": "https://figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/The_Lean_Theorem_Prover_system_description_/6492815"}, {"title": "github.com", "url": "https://github.com/breck7/pldb/blob/main/concepts/lean.scroll"}, {"title": "Lean (proof assistant)", "url": "https://wiki.krisyotam.com/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic_2026-03/Lean_4"}]}, "Yes, the search confirms that Lean was launched in 2013 by Leonardo de Moura at Microsoft Research.\n\n- **Launch:** Sources say the project was started at Microsoft Research in 2013 (The Lean project was launched by Leonardo de Moura at Microsoft Research in 2013.). Wikipedia gives the same date, noting that the early versions, later called Lean 1 and 2, were experimental.\n- **Development:** Wikipedia says de Moura worked on it while at Microsoft Research and later at Amazon Web Services, with contributions from other collaborators.\n- **Early design:** A 2015 Microsoft Research system description calls Lean a new open-source prover with a small trusted kernel based on dependent type theory, developed with Carnegie Mellon University.\n- **Later history:** Lean 4 was released in 2021, and its development is now supported by the nonprofit Lean Focused Research Organization."], "durationSeconds": 5.223953208, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01VotKDtaxZCtmDe1yPaf4L6", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
